    <title>Mathematical Modeling - Fall 2026 - (MTH 305-01) Syllabus</title>

    <introduction>
        <p/>
    </introduction>
    <!-- Include chapters -->
    <subsection>
        <title>Course Information</title>

        <p>
            This is the syllabus for
            <term>Mathematical Modeling (MATH 305, Section 1)</term> for Fall
            2026.
            It is a 3 credit course.
            <!-- use a description list (dl) for short items -->
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Instructor</title>

                    <p>
                        Prof.
                        Regan, MAK C-2-410, 616-331-2045,
                        <url href="mailto:reganmar@gvsu.edu">reganmar@gvsu.edu</url>.
                        You can call me Dr./Prof.
                        Regan or Dr./Prof.
                        Maggie.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The best way to reach me is by email.
                        I will do my best to respond within 24 hours (except
                        weekends).
                        My general working hours are 8 am - 5 pm ET Monday
                        through Friday.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Office Hours</title>

                    <p>
                        Tuesday from 1 - 2 pm ET (MAK A-2-610)
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Wednesday from 11 am - 12 pm ET (MAK A-2-610)
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Thursday from 1 - 2 pm ET (MAK A-2-610)
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        or by appointment
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Class Meets</title>

                    <p>
                        TTh 11:30 am - 12:45 pm ET in MAK A-1-111
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Textbook and Course Materials</title>

                    <p>
                        No textbook -- I will provide notes and worksheets.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        We will use online resources such as
                        <url href="https://colab.research.google.com/">Google Colab</url>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Being Successful in MTH 305</title>

                    <p>
                        My main priority in this course is for you to grow as a
                        mathematician: to be able to understand and apply
                        complex statements, to solve challenging problems, and
                        to communicate orally and in writing.
                        I want you to succeed.
                        My teaching philosophy is that everyone has the right to
                        understand and enjoy mathematics, and to feel part of a
                        mathematical community.
                        Please see me when you need help.
                        Additionally, consider the following things you can do
                        to be successful.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <term>Attend class.</term> Attendance is
                                expected and is correlated to success.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <term>Aim for deep conceptual understanding.</term>
                                Linear algebra makes sense!
                                If you aim for sensemaking the answers (and good
                                grades) will follow.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <term>Use office hours wisely.</term> Office
                                hours are for you to ask questions!
                                Please drop in whenever you have questions!
                                I ask that you prepare by first making your best
                                effort and forming specific questions.
                                This helps our meetings be the most productive.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <term>Talk to your peers.</term> Talking through
                                math can lead to huge insights - try it!
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <term> Please ask for anything that would help your learning. </term>
                                In particular, I know you are human and things
                                come up.
                                Keep me in the loop so I can help.
                                Just email me and we can make an appointment or
                                talk over email.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title> Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion</title>

                    <p>
                        We live in a wonderfully diverse world.
                        Acknowledging and listening to the perspectives and
                        experiences of people from a different cultural
                        background than your own (e.g., gender, race, sexual
                        orientation, nationality, religion, socioeconomic
                        status) is a vital part of the learning process and your
                        personal development.
                        It is my intent that students from all backgrounds and
                        perspectives be well-served by this course, that
                        students' learning needs be addressed both in and out of
                        the classroom, and that the diversity students bring to
                        this class be viewed as a resource, strength, and
                        benefit.
                        All voices need to be heard.
                        I welcome and appreciate any suggestions you have
                        pertaining to diversity, equity, and inclusion, and
                        commit to listen reflectively to your concerns, without
                        defensiveness, in recognition that receiving honest
                        feedback is a necessary step in improving the
                        inclusiveness of our academic environment.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title> Climate Goals</title>

                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <em>Axiom 1.</em> Mathematical potential is
                                distributed equally among different groups,
                                irrespective of geographic, demographic, and
                                economic boundaries.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <em>Axiom 2.</em> Everyone can have joyful,
                                meaningful, and empowering mathematical
                                experiences.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <em>Axiom 3.</em> Mathematics is a powerful,
                                malleable tool that can be shaped and used
                                differently by various communities to serve
                                their needs.
                            </li>

                            <li>
                                <em>Axiom 4.</em> Every student deserves to be
                                treated with dignity and respect.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <!-- Use paragraphs to break apart subsections -->
        <paragraphs>
            <title>What is Mathematical Modeling?</title>

            <p>
                Mathematical modeling is the process of using math to describe,
                analyze, and predict real-world situations.
                Many applications rely on mathematical models within their
                study.
                We will learn how to collect and clean data, how to ask a good
                question, and how to apply the life cycle of mathematical
                modeling in a variety of scenarios, some of which are:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Modeling, representation, and sensitivity – an
                        introduction to how equations can be used to understand
                        real life systems
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Modeling with Calculus – e.g., rates of change,
                        optimization, volume, sequences and series
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Modeling with Differential Equations – e.g., first and
                        second order differential equations, systems of first
                        order differential equations
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Modeling with Linear Algebra – e.g., systems of linear
                        equations, discrete stochastic processes, optimization,
                        data analysis
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>What should you do if you need help?</title>

            <p>
                Successful students reach out for help when they need it.
                You should strive to be aware of when you are struggling.
                Whether you are having a technology issue or a mathematical
                issue or a personal issue impeding your work, reach out to me!
                I'm here to help you be successful in this class.
                If something in your life is preventing you from showing me your
                best work, I would like to help you.
                The earlier you get in touch the easier it is to catch up.
                You can always reach me by email.
                I generally check my email throughout the day but rarely at
                night or on the weekend.
                I'm pretty good about responding to email quickly (and
                definitely within 24 hours during the work week).
                Don't struggle alone!
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </subsection>
    <!-- Other subsections for larger dividers -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-assessmentsM">
        <title>Assessments and Grades</title>

        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Class Preparation [CP] (5%)</title>

                    <p>
                        There will be class preparation assignments due every
                        Tuesday before class (once a week).
                        These are graded for completeness, not correctness.
                        Your grade for this part is the percentage you
                        completed.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Homework [HW] (25%)</title>

                    <p>
                        Several homework problems will be assigned every week
                        involving both coding and written mathematical ideas.
                        These are your chance to show your best work on advanced
                        ideas, including explanation and illustration.
                        All assignments will be submitted and receive feedback
                        on Blackboard.
                        See the HW policies for more information (including info
                        about revisions).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Quizzes [Q] (20%)</title>

                    <p>
                        There will be a quiz each Thursday.
                        These will include questions on recent material --
                        announced the week prior to the quiz.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Labs [L] (20%)</title>

                    <p>
                        Each lab is worth a varying number of points and there
                        will be approximately 3 labs.
                        You can earn partial credit.
                        These will be done in teams.
                        See the lab policies for more information (including
                        info about revisions).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <title>Final Project (30%)</title>

                    <p>
                        The final project will consist of a paper and
                        presentation.
                        More details will be posted on Blackboard with due dates
                        for various components/check-ins.
                        We will start planning early!
                        Most (if not all) presentations will occur during the
                        final exam time:
                        <term>Tuesday, December 15, 10:00 am - 11:50 am ET.</term>
                        As needed, we might have a few presentations the last
                        day of class.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
